 [1999](#page-28-0); Mallakpour & Villarini, [2015](#page-28-1); Pielke Jr. & Downton, [2000](#page-29-2)).","sectionID":"pub:17:sec:0007","sectionTitle":"**1. Introduction**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2022wr032039","sourceUnitID":"pub:17:sec:0007:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"b7f2fa20d3e572b2d69b48728f44c2031730841071d0e462c7b58d5592f5878e","startOffsetInDocument":7538,"startOffsetInUnit":1489},{"endOffsetInDocument":8110,"endOffsetInUnit":2061,"evidenceHash":null,"evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0005","evidenceText":"Some are expecting that the hydrologic cycle will intensify due to climate change which will lead to more extreme precipitation in some areas along with a greater risk of flooding (Milly et al., [2002;](#page-29-3) Tabari, [2020;](#page-30-2) Wing et al., [2018](#page-30-3)).","sectionID":"pub:17:sec:0007","sectionTitle":"**1. Introduction**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2022wr032039","sourceUnitID":"pub:17:sec:0007:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"b7f2fa20d3e572b2d69b48728f44c2031730841071d0e462c7b58d5592f5878e","startOffsetInDocument":7834,"startOffsetInUnit":1785},{"endOffsetInDocument":8399,"endOffsetInUnit":2350,"evidenceHash":null,"evidenceSpanID":"evidence-0006","evidenceText":"Increasing trends in frequency and risk are not uniform across spatial regions with work by Slater and Villarini [\\(2016](#page-30-4)) indicating that trends are increasing across the US Midwest and Great Lakes regions while decreasing in the coastal Southeast, Southwest, and California.","sectionID":"pub:17:sec:0007","sectionTitle":"**1. Introduction**","sourceArtifactID":"https://doi.org/10.1029/2022wr032039","sourceUnitID":"pub:17:sec:0007:unit:0001","sourceUnitTextHash":"b7f2fa20d3e572b2d69b48728f44c2031730841071d0e462c7b58d5592f5878e","startOffsetInDocument":8111,"startOffsetInUnit":2062}],"metadata":{"candidateSchemaSha256":"50132ce01a16a21736f65e4b5d4b0354b3d1c53f07878352159d6ff36e94fce2","candidateSchemaVersion":"0.1.0","contextSour